.Dd September 22, 2026
.Dt EMUXFS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm emuxfs
.Nd mirroring, checksumming, and self-healing filesystem layer
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm emuxfs
.Cm format
.Op Fl a Ar checksum_algorithm
.Ar directory ...
.Nm emuxfs
.Cm mount
.Op Fl f
.Ar mount_point
.Ar directory ...
.Nm emuxfs
.Cm audit
.Ar directory ...
.Nm emuxfs
.Cm heal
.Ar directory ...
.Nm emuxfs
.Cm sync
.Ar destination
.Ar source ...
.Nm emuxfs
.Cm version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a mirroring, checksumming, and self-healing filesystem layer for
.Ox .
It mirrors a filesystem tree across a series of directories, and employs
checksum databases to validate and restore files automatically.
.Pp
The Enhanced Multiplexed File System
.Pq emuxfs
is authored by David Uhden Collado.
It began as a fork of the original Multiplexed File System by
Stephen D. Adams;
his copyright notices are retained.
The executable is
.Nm ,
the manual page is
.Pa emuxfs.1 ,
the private on-disk directory is
.Pa .muxfs ,
and internal symbols use the
.Va emuxfs_
prefix.
.Pp
In addition to the file content, metadata is checksummed, including: owner,
group, mode, (external) inode number, and for regular files the file size.
For directories the checksummed content includes the filenames and
corresponding checksums of the files in the directory.
For symbolic links the checksummed content is the path output by
.Xr readlink 2 .
.Pp
The on-disk layout is versioned by a
.Va format_version
key in
.Pa .muxfs/muxfs.conf .
Arrays written before the key existed have the version 1 layout and are
accepted as such.
An array whose format version is not supported is refused rather than
mounted, and is never migrated automatically.
.Sh COMMANDS
During an
.Cm audit ,
.Cm heal ,
or
.Cm sync
the
.Nm
array must not be mounted.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm format Oo Fl a Ar checksum_algorithm Oc Ar directory ...
Format the directories as part of a new
.Nm
array.
The
.Ar checksum_algorithm
may be
.Cm crc32 ,
.Cm md5 ,
or
.Cm sha1 ,
and defaults to
.Cm md5 .
The directories must be empty and non-overlapping.
.It Cm mount Oo Fl f Oc Ar mount_point Ar directory ...
Mount the directories as a redundant array at
.Ar mount_point .
If
.Fl f
is specified then
.Nm
remains in the foreground.
.It Cm audit Ar directory ...
If the directories are out-of-sync write a warning to the standard error and
exit.
Otherwise, for each
.Ar directory ,
read and check the checksums of all filesystem nodes, and write to the
standard output the paths of the filesystem nodes that do not match.
.Cm audit
is read-only: it does not modify the array or its private metadata, so it is
safe to use for inspection and forensics.
It performs no explicit writes; reading the backing files can still update
their access times, depending on the mount options of the underlying
filesystem.
.It Cm heal Ar directory ...
Perform an
.Cm audit
and attempt to restore the filesystem nodes found to be corrupted using the
corresponding copies in the other directories.
If two or more devices hold internally valid but different copies of a node,
nothing is overwritten and
.Cm heal
exits non-zero; resolve the ambiguity explicitly with
.Cm sync .
.It Cm sync Ar destination Ar source ...
Restore the directory
.Ar destination
to match the state of the
.Ar source
directories.
.Cm sync
is the explicit recovery path: it reopens
.Ar destination
even if it is marked as having an interrupted operation, rebuilds it from
.Ar source ,
and clears the interrupted-operation state so the array can be mounted again.
.It Cm version
Display the program version and exit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with one of the following exit status values:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent -compact
.It 0
success
.It 1
incorrect usage
.It 255
error
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create a new
.Nm
array from the filesystems mounted at
.Pa /mnt/a
and
.Pa /mnt/b
using the
.Cm sha1
checksum algorithm:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# emuxfs format -a sha1 /mnt/a /mnt/b
.Ed
.Pp
Mount the
.Nm
array consisting of directories
.Pa /mnt/a
and
.Pa /mnt/b
to the mount-point
.Pa /mnt/mp :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# emuxfs mount /mnt/mp /mnt/a /mnt/b
.Ed
.Pp
Unmount the
.Nm
array:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# umount /mnt/mp
.Ed
.Pp
To perform an
.Cm audit
or
.Cm heal
operation the array must not be mounted.
.Pp
Audit the
.Nm
array:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# emuxfs audit /mnt/a /mnt/b
.Ed
.Pp
Heal the
.Nm
array:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# emuxfs heal /mnt/a /mnt/b
.Ed
.Pp
Re-sync corrupted
.Pa /mnt/b
to state of
.Pa /mnt/a
after loss of power:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# emuxfs sync /mnt/b /mnt/a
.Ed
.Pp
Create or update a backup of the array to
.Pa /mnt/c ,
the mount-point of a filesystem on a detachable disk:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# emuxfs sync /mnt/c /mnt/a /mnt/b
.Ed
.Sh HISTORY
The original muxfs (the Multiplexed File System) was written by Stephen D.
Adams.
The Enhanced Multiplexed File System
.Pq emuxfs
is derived from it.
The private on-disk directory
.Pa .muxfs
and its configuration file
.Pa muxfs.conf
keep their historical names so that arrays created by muxfs remain usable.
The on-disk layout is unchanged apart from the addition of the
.Va format_version
key.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An David Uhden Collado Aq Mt daviduhden@gmail.com
.An Stephen D. Adams Aq Mt stephen@sdadams.org
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
supports regular files, directories, and symbolic links, and does not support
special files, device nodes, file locking, or hard-linking.
Hard links are detected and refused rather than silently misrepresented.
The checksummed metadata covers the node type, permissions, owner, group, the
external inode number mapping, the content, directory entry sets and symbolic
link targets.
It does not cover
.Xr timespec 3
timestamps,
.Xr chflags 2
flags, or extended attributes.
When several devices hold internally valid but different copies of a path,
automatic repair is refused and must be resolved explicitly with
.Cm sync .
.Pp
Filesystem nodes named
.Pa .muxfs
are reserved for use by
.Nm .
.Pp
.Nm
relies on the uniqueness of inode numbers within each directory to perform
correctly, therefore the mounting of filesystems within array directories is
not supported, with the exception of the
.Pa .muxfs
directories.
.Pp
To mitigate hard drive head thrashing the top-level
.Pa .muxfs
directories may be moved to solid state storage then mounted back to their
original locations.
.Pp
.Nm
relies on writing sequentially to multiple directories to mitigate corruption
during unexpected of loss of power.
After such an event the administrator must
.Cm sync
the array from one or more of the uncorrupted directories before mounting it.
A device that did not complete an operation, or whose sequence number is
exhausted, is marked as needing attention and cannot be mounted;
.Cm sync
clears an interrupted operation but a device marked
.Sq degraded
by a hardware, integrity or sequence-exhaustion fault must be reformatted.
Due to this it is not recommended to use emuxfs with only one directory in the
array.
.Pp
For privacy between users
.Xr syslog 3
messages are sent to the
.Sq user
facility.
To receive these messages an entry must be set up in
.Xr syslog.conf 5 .
Ensure that the corresponding log file is not readable by other.
The log messages include notifications of files found to be corrupted and the
success or failure of file restoration attempts.
.Pp
The size of the
.Nm
database is proportional to the value of the largest (internal) inode number
encountered.
To minimize this dedicate whole filesystems to the
.Nm
array.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.Nm
is confined with
.Xr pledge 2
and
.Xr unveil 2
to the directories named on the command line.
After configuration the filesystem view is locked and cannot be widened.
The checksums detect accidental corruption only; they are not
cryptographic and do not protect against a writer who can modify a device
directly.
See
.Pa SECURITY.md
in the source distribution for the full threat model and the per-command
promise policy.
